    tags: [
      "best soy protein powder",
      "soy protein powder",
      "soy protein isolate",
      "soy protein isolate powder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Buyer's Guide" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Soy Protein Powder</Fragment>
      Soy is the cheapest complete plant protein you can buy and one of the most studied. Here are the isolates worth buying, ranked on what the label actually delivers, plus a straight answer on the testosterone question.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#how-we-ranked">How We Ranked</a>
      <a href="#soy-vs-whey">Soy vs Whey for Muscle</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#testosterone">Soy and Testosterone</a>
      <a href="#isolate-types">Isolate vs Concentrate</a>
      <a href="#dosing">How Much per Shake</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#safety">Allergy, Thyroid, Metals</a>
      <a href="#gmo-hexane">GMO, Hexane, Organic</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy Soy</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Soy protein isolate is the most underrated powder on the shelf: a complete protein, roughly 90 percent protein by dry weight, cheaper per gram than most pea and nearly all whey, and backed by trials showing muscle gains on par with whey when the dose is right. The worry that keeps people away from it, that soy lowers testosterone, has been tested in dozens of clinical studies and does not hold up at normal intakes.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-ranked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Ranked These Soy Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            Soy protein powder is a small category on Amazon, and a lot of what shows up for the search is not soy at all. Many of the top sellers for the phrase are pea and rice blends from plant brands, and a few are whey. We started by throwing out anything where soy was not the main protein source on the ingredient list, which left a short list of unflavored isolates, a handful of flavored isolates and a couple of soy-based meal shakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            From there we ranked on four things. First, protein per serving and protein per gram of powder, because a 10 g scoop and a 27 g scoop are not comparable until you put them on the same basis. Second, independent testing: a sport certification such as Informed Sport carries more weight than a brand saying its own batches are tested. Third, the ingredient list, where shorter is generally better. Fourth, cost per 25 g of protein at typical prices.
          </p>

          <p>
            We did not hand out points for marketing claims about isoflavones, hormones or weight loss, and we did not use star ratings, because they say more about flavor and shipping than about what is in the tub. Where a figure differed between listings, as it does for some bulk brands, we give the range and tell you to check the label. Where we could not confirm a figure at all, we left it out rather than guess.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical note: almost every pure soy isolate here is unflavored. That is not an oversight. Flavored soy shakes largely disappeared from the US market as whey and pea took over, so if you want chocolate or vanilla, your realistic options are a smaller flavored isolate, a soy-based meal shake, or an unflavored isolate blended with fruit, cocoa or a spoon of nut butter.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Criterion</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What we checked</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why it matters</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein density</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Grams of protein per gram of powder</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Separates real isolates from filler-heavy blends</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Testing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Informed Sport, NSF, or brand lab claims</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plant proteins carry more heavy-metal risk</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ingredients</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Soy first, few additives</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fewer sweeteners and gums, fewer surprises</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost per 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Typical price divided by total protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The only fair way to compare tub sizes</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="soy-vs-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Does Soy Build Muscle as Well as Whey?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mostly, yes, once the dose is matched. Soy is a complete protein with all nine essential amino acids, and soy protein isolate scores the maximum 1.0 on the older PDCAAS scale used for food labels. On the newer DIAAS scale it lands a little below whey, mainly because whey is unusually rich in leucine, the amino acid that does the most to switch on muscle protein synthesis after a meal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The leucine gap is real but small. Whey protein runs around 10 to 11 percent leucine, while soy runs closer to 8 percent. In practice that means a 25 g scoop of whey delivers roughly the leucine of 30 to 32 g of soy protein. If you are using soy as your main post-workout shake, take a slightly bigger serving, or pair it with a meal, and the difference largely disappears.
          </p>

          <p>
            The long-term trials back that up. A 2018 meta-analysis by Messina and colleagues in the International Journal of Sport Nutrition and Exercise Metabolism pooled resistance-training studies that compared soy supplements with animal protein supplements and found no significant difference in gains in lean mass or strength. The authors did note the trials were small and mostly short, which is typical for this kind of research.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most cited head-to-head is a 2021 study by Hevia-Larraín and colleagues in Sports Medicine. Previously untrained vegans and omnivores ate about 1.6 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per day for 12 weeks while lifting, with the vegans topping up using soy protein isolate and the omnivores using whey. Both groups gained similar amounts of leg lean mass, muscle size and strength.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where soy falls short is not muscle but taste and texture. Soy isolate is thicker and beanier than whey in plain water, and most buyers end up blending it. If that is acceptable, soy is one of the best value ways to hit a daily protein target, whether you are plant-based or simply trying to spend less. For a wider plant comparison, see our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide and our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> breakdown.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Soy Protein Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            Five soy powders from five brands, all sold on Amazon US at the time of writing. Label figures come from the brand or retailer listing and can change between batches and flavors, so check the panel on the tub you actually buy. We have not tasted these ourselves; anything about flavor or mixing is buyer feedback.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="testosterone" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Soy and Testosterone: What the Research Actually Shows
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the question that keeps most men away from soy, so it deserves a straight answer. Soy contains isoflavones, mainly genistein and daidzein, which are plant compounds that can bind to estrogen receptors. That is where the phytoestrogen label comes from, and it is a reasonable thing to wonder about. But binding to a receptor in a lab dish is not the same as changing hormone levels in a person eating normal amounts.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best evidence is a 2021 meta-analysis by Reed and colleagues in Reproductive Toxicology, which pooled dozens of clinical studies in men and found that neither soy protein nor isoflavone intake significantly affected total testosterone, free testosterone, estradiol or estrone, even at intakes well above what a typical Japanese diet provides. An earlier 2010 meta-analysis by Hamilton-Reeves and colleagues in Fertility and Sterility reached the same conclusion.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two pieces of evidence get passed around as proof of harm, and both need context. One is a small 2013 study by Kraemer and colleagues in which ten resistance-trained men taking soy for 14 days showed a slightly blunted testosterone response after workouts compared with whey. It was short, tiny and measured acute post-exercise responses, and the longer muscle-growth trials above show no penalty in actual results.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other is a handful of case reports, the best known a 2008 report by Martinez and Lewi in Endocrine Practice of a 60-year-old man who developed breast tissue growth while drinking about three quarts of soy milk a day. His symptoms resolved after he stopped. That is an extreme, sustained intake, many times what one or two scoops of soy isolate provides, and it is the kind of exception that does not change the overall picture.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT ANSWER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">One or two soy shakes a day will not lower your testosterone.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Pooled clinical trials in men show no meaningful change in testosterone or estrogen from soy protein or isoflavones at normal intakes. The case reports of feminizing effects involve extreme, sustained consumption. If you have a hormone-sensitive condition or take hormone therapy, ask your doctor; this is not medical advice.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Isoflavone content also varies by how the isolate is made. Water-washed isolates keep more of their isoflavones, while alcohol-washed isolates lose a large share in processing. Most brands do not say which method they use or list isoflavones per scoop, so do not expect to find it on the label. If you want to minimize isoflavones for any reason, a serving or two a day still keeps you in the range of ordinary soy-food intake.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="isolate-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Isolate, Concentrate and Textured Soy: What You Are Buying
          </h2>

          <p>
            Soy protein comes in three main forms. Soy flour is the least processed, around 50 percent protein, and is not sold as a protein powder. Soy protein concentrate is around 70 percent protein, with more carbohydrate and fiber left in. Soy protein isolate is the most refined, defined in the industry as at least 90 percent protein on a dry basis, and it is what every product on our list uses as its main ingredient.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can check this yourself with simple division. NOW Sports lists 20 g of protein in a 24 g scoop, about 83 percent protein as sold. BulkSupplements lists roughly 25 to 27 g in a 30 g serving, and Nutricost lists 10 g in an 11 g unflavored scoop. Those ratios are exactly what an isolate with a bit of moisture should give. Flavored versions drop lower because cocoa, sweetener and flavoring take up weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a soy powder claims more protein per gram than an isolate can physically contain, be suspicious. A flavored product listing 30 g of protein in a 31 g scoop, for example, does not add up. That kind of mismatch can point to rounding, a mislabeled scoop size or, in the worst case, added free amino acids that inflate the protein test. We cover that problem in detail in our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Textured soy protein, sold as TVP, is a different product: a dried, extruded soy flour or concentrate used as a meat substitute. It is cheap and useful in cooking, but it is not a shake powder. Likewise, soy milk powders and soy-based meal shakes contain soy protein alongside sugar, fat and added nutrients, so their protein per gram is much lower than an isolate's, and they are priced accordingly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Soy Protein per Shake
          </h2>

          <p>
            For muscle building, aim for about 30 to 35 g of soy protein per shake if the shake is doing the job of a meal or a post-workout feed. That gets you to roughly 2.5 g of leucine, the amount most researchers treat as a practical threshold for a strong muscle-building response. If the shake sits alongside a protein-rich meal, a standard 20 to 25 g serving is fine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Translated into scoops, that is about one and a half scoops of NOW Sports, a little over one serving of BulkSupplements or Horbaach, and three scoops of Nutricost, whose 10 g scoop is unusually small. Soylent's 30 g serving reaches the target in one go. Always weigh a scoop once on a kitchen scale; scoop sizes and powder density vary, and the label serving is measured by weight, not by how full the scoop looks.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~8%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Leucine in soy protein</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">30-35 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Soy protein to match 25 g whey</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1.0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Soy isolate PDCAAS</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1.6 g/kg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Daily protein in the vegan vs omnivore trial</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Daily totals matter more than any single shake. Most active adults do well between 1.4 and 2.0 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per day, per the International Society of Sports Nutrition position stand, and powder should fill gaps rather than replace food. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide walks through the maths for your body weight and goals.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing is flexible. A soy shake after training is convenient, but total protein spread across three or four meals does more than squeezing everything into a narrow window. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant, or have been told to limit protein, agree your daily target with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding shakes.
          </p>
        </div>

