        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Allergies, Thyroid, Heavy Metals and When to Ask a Doctor
          </h2>

          <p>
            Soy is one of the nine major food allergens in the United States, and soy allergy is common in young children, though many outgrow it. If you react to soy foods, soy protein isolate is not a safe workaround, because the protein is the allergen. People with a peanut allergy sometimes ask about soy too; cross-reactivity is possible but not the rule, so ask an allergist rather than testing it yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thyroid questions come up often. Research reviews have generally found that soy does not cause hypothyroidism in people with normal thyroid function and adequate iodine intake. The more practical issue is medication: soy can interfere with the absorption of levothyroxine, so people on thyroid medication are usually advised to separate the two. Ask your doctor or pharmacist how far apart to take them; this is not medical advice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Breast cancer is the other common worry. The American Cancer Society and the American Institute for Cancer Research both say that eating traditional soy foods is safe for breast cancer survivors and may even be beneficial. Evidence on concentrated isoflavone supplements is thinner, and a protein isolate sits somewhere in between, so anyone with a hormone-sensitive cancer should discuss soy powders with their oncology team.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heavy metals are a plant-protein issue generally, not a soy-specific one. Crops take up lead, cadmium and arsenic from soil, and independent testing has found plant powders tend to carry more lead on average than whey. Nutricost's soy isolate and Soylent's powder both carry California Proposition 65 lead warnings on their listings, which reflect a very low threshold rather than proof of harm, but are worth knowing about.
          </p>

          <p>
            If metals concern you, a sport certification like NOW Sports' Informed Sport status adds a layer of independent screening, though it focuses on banned substances rather than metals. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> guide covers what each certification does and does not test for, and how to request batch results from a brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gmo-hexane" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. GMO, Hexane and Organic: Label Worries Sorted
          </h2>

          <p>
            The large majority of soybeans grown in the US are genetically engineered, which is why so many soy powders advertise non-GMO status. NOW Sports carries Non-GMO Project Verification on its soy isolate, and several other brands describe their soy as non-GMO or identity preserved, meaning the beans were kept separate from conventional supply. Major scientific bodies regard approved GM crops as safe to eat, so this is a preference rather than a safety rule.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hexane is the other label worry. Conventional soybean processing uses the solvent hexane to extract oil, and protein isolates are made from the defatted flakes left over. The finished isolate goes through further washing and drying, and residues are generally considered trace. If you would rather avoid the issue altogether, USDA organic rules do not allow hexane-extracted organic ingredients, but certified organic soy isolate is hard to find in shake form.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gums, lecithin and sweeteners are worth a glance too. Unflavored isolates usually list nothing but soy protein isolate. Flavored versions add cocoa, natural flavors, salt and a sweetener; Nutricost's vanilla, for example, lists stevia and a few grams of added sugar per scoop. Soylent sweetens with allulose and adds MCT oil, vitamins and minerals, which makes it closer to a light meal than a plain protein shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of these issues should outweigh the basics. A soy powder that delivers the protein it claims, comes from a brand that tests its batches, and fits your budget is a good buy whether or not it carries every badge. Pay for organic or non-GMO if those matter to you, but do not pay a premium for them believing they make the protein itself work better.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Soy Protein, and Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Soy makes the most sense for three groups. Vegans and vegetarians who want a complete protein with no blending tricks; people on a tight budget who want the cheapest honest gram of protein in powder form; and anyone who cannot tolerate dairy but finds pea protein too earthy or too gritty. For all three, an unflavored isolate in a smoothie is about as efficient as protein gets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Soy also suits people who already eat tofu, tempeh and soy milk and like the taste. If you are new to it, buy the smallest size first. Soy isolate in water is an acquired taste for many buyers, who report a thick, slightly beany shake; blended with frozen fruit, oats or cocoa, most of those complaints go away. If you want a pea option instead, see our <a href="/best-pea-protein-powder/">best pea protein powder</a> rankings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip soy if you have a soy allergy, obviously, and talk to your doctor first if you take thyroid medication, have a hormone-sensitive cancer, or are pregnant. Skip it too if you will only drink a shake that tastes like a dessert; the flavored soy options are limited, and you will likely be happier with a well-reviewed whey or pea blend. Hemp is another plant option worth a look in our <a href="/best-hemp-protein-powder/">best hemp protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most buyers, our recommendation is simple. Start with NOW Sports Soy Protein Isolate if you value independent testing, or BulkSupplements if cost per gram is the priority. Blend it, take a slightly bigger serving than you would of whey, and ignore the hormone scare stories. The evidence on soy is better than its reputation, and the price is hard to beat.
          </p>
        </div>

