              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 25 g scoop of soy has a little less leucine than the same amount of whey. If the shake is your main post-workout feed, take 30 to 35 g of soy protein, or pair a smaller scoop with a protein-rich meal, so you are not shortchanging the muscle-building signal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a meal shake by mistake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many products that appear in a soy protein search are pea blends, whey, or soy-based meal replacements with sugar and only 13 to 15 g of protein. Read the ingredient list and the protein per serving before you buy, not the product photo or the word soy in the title.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing tubs instead of protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cheap tub of 10 g scoops can cost more per gram than a pricier tub of 25 g scoops. Divide the price by the total grams of protein in the container, then multiply by 25, and you will see which one is actually the better deal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Drinking unflavored isolate in water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plain soy isolate in water is thick and beany, and buyers who try it that way often give up. Blend it with frozen fruit, oats, cocoa or nut butter, or stir it into yogurt or oatmeal, where the taste largely disappears.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring medication interactions</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Soy can interfere with the absorption of levothyroxine and may matter for hormone-sensitive conditions. If you take thyroid medication, are being treated for cancer, or are pregnant, ask your doctor before adding a daily soy shake rather than relying on what you read online.
              </p>
