        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does soy protein lower testosterone?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not at normal intakes. A 2021 meta-analysis in Reproductive Toxicology pooled dozens of clinical studies in men and found no significant effect of soy protein or isoflavones on testosterone or estrogen. Case reports of feminizing effects involve extreme intakes, such as about three quarts of soy milk a day. One or two soy shakes daily is well within the studied range.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is soy protein as good as whey for building muscle?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Close to it, once the dose is matched. Soy has slightly less leucine, so 30 to 35 g of soy protein roughly matches a 25 g whey scoop. Meta-analyses of resistance-training trials have found no significant difference in lean mass or strength gains, and a 2021 trial found vegans using soy gained as much as omnivores using whey.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is soy protein a complete protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Soy contains all nine essential amino acids in useful amounts, and soy protein isolate scores the maximum 1.0 on the PDCAAS scale used for food labeling. Its limiting amino acid is methionine, which is lower than in dairy or eggs, but a varied diet covers that easily. It is one of very few plant proteins that is complete on its own.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the difference between soy protein isolate and concentrate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Isolate is the most refined form, at least 90 percent protein on a dry basis, with most carbohydrate and fat removed. Concentrate is around 70 percent protein and keeps more carbohydrate and fiber. Isolate gives more protein per calorie and mixes more smoothly, which is why every powder on this list uses it as the main ingredient.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is soy protein safe for women?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most women, yes. Soy foods are a staple in many diets, and major cancer organizations say traditional soy foods are safe for breast cancer survivors. Evidence on concentrated isoflavone supplements is thinner, so women with hormone-sensitive cancers, those who are pregnant, and anyone on hormone therapy should discuss soy powders with their doctor first.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can soy protein affect my thyroid?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Research reviews generally find soy does not cause thyroid problems in people with normal thyroid function and enough iodine. The practical concern is medication: soy can reduce the absorption of levothyroxine. If you take thyroid medication, ask your doctor or pharmacist how far apart to take your dose and your soy shake. This is not medical advice.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the cheapest soy protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Unflavored bulk isolates are the cheapest. At typical prices, BulkSupplements Soy Protein Isolate works out to roughly $0.75 to $0.90 per 25 g of protein, with NOW Sports close behind and adding Informed Sport testing. Prices change often, so compare cost per 25 g of protein, not price per tub, before you buy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
